<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Angola</h1><p class="st-meta">1975 CE – present · capital Luanda</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Portuguese-speaking country on Africa's south-west coast, independent in 1975 and followed by a civil war until 2002, ruled since by the MPLA.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola</a> became independent in 1975 after a war that began in 1961 and the fall of Portugal's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnation-revolution">Carnation Revolution</a> of 1974. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvor-agreement">Alvor Agreement</a> of January 1975 set independence for 11 November 1975 and called for a coalition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpla">MPLA</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-of-angola">FNLA</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unita">UNITA</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The three movements fell out before independence and fought a civil war, in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpla">MPLA</a>, backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, drove its opponents from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a>. Between 1975 and 1991 the MPLA ran a Marxist-Leninist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>, then in 1990 declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-democracy">social democracy</a> its platform. A peace agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unita">UNITA</a> in 1991 led to elections in 1992, and UNITA returned to war after the MPLA won. The government killed Jonas Savimbi on 22 February 2002, and the Luena Memorandum ended the war.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The constitution of 2010 provides that the president and vice-president of the party that wins the parliamentary elections become president and vice-president. José Eduardo dos Santos stepped down in 2017 after 38 years and was succeeded by João Lourenço. In August 2022 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpla">MPLA</a> won again and Lourenço won a second five-year term in the tightest election in Angola's history.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/angola.jpg" alt="Angola" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Original: Henrique de Carvalho Santos Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Angola.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1975 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Luanda</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>José Eduardo dos Santos, João Lourenço</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>mostly Christian (Catholic and Protestant), indigenous beliefs</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese, Umbundu, Kimbundu, Kikongo</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/angola">Angola</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Angola</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2000&amp;focus=angola" aria-label="Angola on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Angola lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g00000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6108 5751L6108 5753"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6108 5753h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6108 5751h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6108 5751h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Luanda. <a href="/atlas?year=2000&amp;focus=angola">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
